    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen a number ends in a zero, it's easy to express that number in terms of tens.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's consider the following number:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 15{\\color{blue}{0}}]\n\nContent:\nNotice that this number ends in a zero. Therefore, we can write it as a multiple of [MATH: 10] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 15{\\color{blue}{0}} = 15 \\times 1{\\color{blue}{0}}]\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/ad783d5a0dcd12dbfdd6862f07fb7eba]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, [MATH: 150] is the same as \"[MATH: 15] tens.\"\n\nContent:\nIn the future, we'll see how this method can be used to solve problems involving multiplication and division easily.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Rewriting a Number Ending in One Zero\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: 5,860] in terms of tens.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe know that\n[MATH: 5,860 = 586 \\times 10.]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 5,860] is the same as [MATH: 586] tens.\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 560] is the same as\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 56] tens\nb. [MATH: 560] hundreds\nc. [MATH: 560] tens\nd. [MATH: 56] thousands\ne. [MATH: 56] ones\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7, 920] is the same as\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7, 920] tens\nb. [MATH: 792] tens\nc. [MATH: 792] hundreds\nd. [MATH: 7, 920] hundreds\ne. [MATH: 79, 200] tens\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Tutorial: Writing a Number in Terms of Hundreds\n\nContent:\nWhen a number ends in two zeros, we can easily express it in terms of hundreds.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's consider the following number:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 6,2{\\color{blue}{00}}]\n\nContent:\nNotice that this number ends in two zeros. Therefore, we can write it as a multiple of [MATH: 100] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 6,2{\\color{blue}{00}} = 62 \\times 1{\\color{blue}{00}}.]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, [MATH: 6,200] is the same as \"[MATH: 62] hundreds.\"\n\nContent:\nSimilarly, when a number ends in three zeros, it's easy to express it in terms of thousands. Let's see an example.\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Rewriting a Number Ending in Two or Three Zeros\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: 99,000] in terms of thousands?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe know that\n[MATH: 99,000 = 99 \\times 1,000.]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 99,000] is the same as [MATH: 99] thousands.\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3, 600] is the same as\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 360] ones\nb. [MATH: 3, 600] hundreds\nc. [MATH: 36] tens\nd. [MATH: 36] hundreds\ne. [MATH: 360] hundreds\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 72, 000] is the same as\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 720] thousands\nb. [MATH: 72] tens\nc. [MATH: 720] tens\nd. [MATH: 72] hundreds\ne. [MATH: 72] thousands"
